title: Atrioventricular Conduction Block
---

# Atrioventricular Conduction Block

- **Definition** - AV block is a bradyarrhythmia defined as impaired electrical conduction from the atria and ventricles
- **Electrocardiographic definitions of AV conduction block** - based on ECG morphology (rhythm and interval analysis) but also reflects location of block along AV conduction axis: 
![](../img/electrocardiographic_definition_of_av_block.png)
  - <u>1st-degree AV block</u> - conduction delay occuring in the compact AV node, but all impulses are conducted to the ventricle:
    - Ocassionally atrium or His bundle affected
    - Prolonged PR interval (\> 200 ms)
    - Narrow QRS complex
  - <u>2nd-degree AV block</u> - intermittent failure of conduction between atria and ventricles: 
  ![](../img/mobitz_1_vs_2_block.png)
  - <u>3rd-degree AV block</u> - complete heart block w/ AV dissociation where V rate \< A rate: 
  ![](../img/mobitz_two_vs_chb.png)
- **Etiology of AV block:** 
![](../img/causes_of_av_bllock_harrisons.png)
- **Idiopathic fibrosis and degeneration of the conduction system:**
  - <u>Lev's disease</u> - senile degeneration of the conduction system, particularly resulting in **proximal bundle branch fibrosis**
  - <u>Lenegre's disease</u> - sclerodegenerative process in younger patients, typically involving more **distal portions of the bundle branhces**
  - <u>Calcificaiton of the valvular annulus</u> - often associated w/ aortic valve annulus, while mitral valve annulus involvement is less common
- **Iatrogenic cause of AV block:**
  - <u>Rate limting surgeries</u> (N.B. most have underlying AV block) - beta-blockers, non-DHP CCBs, digoxin
  - <u>Transcatheter interventions</u> - percutaneous TAVR, alcohol septal ablation, septal myectomy, ablation for AVNRTs
  - <u>Surgical interventions</u> - valvular surgeries, septal myectomy, surgical repairs of septal defects and transposition of the great arteries
- **AV block in the setting of myocardial ischaemia** - due to 1) direct ischaemia of the conduction system, and 2) enhanced vagal tone (Bezold-Jarisch reflex), where type of block may be a/w different coronary territories:
  - <u>Inferior MI</u> - level of block proximal to bundle of His (90%), often w/ reliable stable junctional escape rhythms (narrow complexes)
  - <u>Anterior AI</u> - level of block distal involving His bundle and bundle branches (CHB possibly preceeded by RBBB due to ischaemia), often w/ less reliable ventricular escape rhythms (wide complex)
- **Infectious causes of AV block:**
  - <u>Lyme carditis</u> (Borrelia burgdorferi) - typically at level of AV node w/ junctional escape rhythm at \> 40 bpm, typically resolves after 1 week of ABx therapy
  - <u>Infective endocarditis</u> - presence often indicates **perivalvular abscess**, which requires urgent surgical intervention
  - <u>Other infectious disorders</u> - Chagas' disease, toxoplasmosis, viral myocarditis
- **Infiltrative causes of AV block:**
  - <u>Rheumatological disorders</u> - **systemic sclerosis** (conduction system fibrosis), other CTDs (e.g. SLE, RA, mCTD)
  - <u>Other infiltrative diseases</u>:
    - Sarcoidosis
    - Cardiac amyloidosis
    - Haemochromatosis
- **Functional causes of AV block:**
  - <u>Electrolyte disturbances</u> - **hyperK**, hypoK, hypoMg
  - <u>Endocrinopathies</u> - severe hypothyroidism, hypoaldosteronism
  - <u>Cardioneurogenic syncopes</u> (increased vagal tone) - vasovagal syncope, carotid sinus hypersensitivity, situational syncope
  - <u>Other excessive vagal tones</u> - sleep apnea
- **Neuromuscular disorders a/w AV block** - accelerated muscule loss and fibrosis:
  - Nucleotide repeat disease myotonic dystrophy
  - Mitochondrial myopathy Kearns-Sayre syndrome
  - Other monogenic muscular dystrophies
- **Congenital causes of AV block:**
  - <u>Maternal lupus</u> - typically a/w maternal anti-Ro +ve disease
  - <u>Complex congenital hearat diseases</u> - transposition of great arteries, ostium primum ASDs, VSDs, endocardial cushion defects, single ventricle defects
  - <u>Others</u> - idiopathic congenital AV block in structurally normal heart
- **Approach to the patient w/ known AV block:** 
![](../img/evaluation_of_patient_with_suspected_av_block.png)
  - <u>Hx</u> - evidence of haemodynamic comprimise, known reversible causes (e.g. medications, sleep apnea)
  - <u>P/E</u> - evaluation of the presence or absence of strucural heart disease
  - <u>Initial Ix</u> - ECG and echocardiography for detection of other conduction abnormalities and structural heart disease
  - <u>Additional evaluation</u>:
    - Advanced imaging - Cardiac CT, cardiac MRI, PET for infiltrative heart disease
    - Ischaemia workup - driven by clinical suspicion at presentation (e.g. Sx and ECG of ischaemia)
    - Execise testing and electrophysiology studies - delineate the level of block
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Determine the rhythm</u> - 1st-degree block, 2nd-degree block, CHB
    - <u>Identify and eliminate reversible causes</u> - e.g. drugs, ischaemia, electrolytes, and excessive vagal tone
    - <u>Evaluation of the safety and reliability of escape rhythms</u> - guides the need for pacing; as rule of thumb, the lower the escape rhythm, the less reliable it is
    - <u>Pharmacological therapy</u> - only for low-level blocks likely originate from AV node
    - <u>Temporary pacing</u> - trancutaneous/ transvenous pacing based on 1) Sx, 2) haemodynamic status, and 3) estimated level of the block
    - <u>Permanent pacing</u> - typically required if irreversible infranodal AV blocks

    
    ![](../img/approach_to_mx_of_av_block.png)
  - **Identify and eliminate reversible causes:**
    - <u>Unnecessary medications known to delay AV conduction</u> - beta-blockers, diltiaem, verapamil, digoxin
    - <u>Electrolyte disturbances</u> - hyperK, hypoK, hypoMg
    - <u>Ischaemia</u> - principally reversal of the ischaemia will reverse AV block, but certain MIs are a/w more significant AV blocks:
      - Inferior MI - narrow complex junctional rhythm; haemodynamically stable
      - Anterior MI - wide complex ventricular rhythm; haemodynamically unstable often requires temporary pacing
    - <u>Infiltrative diseases</u> - immunosuppression improves cardiac sarcoidosis
  - **Transcutaneous pacing** - patches over cardiac apex (cathode), and above right nipple (anode):
    - Highly effective once electrical and mechanical capture attained
    - Duration of Tx limited to patient discomfort and longer-term failre to capture the ventricle due to changes in lead impedance
  - **Transvenous pacing:**
    - Advanced pacing wire via jugular, subclavian, or femoral venous system
    - Permits stable temprary pacing for haemodynamically significant reversible blocks
  - **Pacemaker innplantation:**
    - **Indications** - principally if 1) Sx, 2) high-degree block, or 3) expected progression of conduction system disease (see details above):
      - <u>Low-degree AV block</u> - Sx or known condunction system disease (e.g. neuromuscular disease, Lamin A/C cardiomyopathy)
      - <u>High-degree AV block</u> (CHB, mobitz II) - indicated for all, irrespective of Sx (if irreversible)
    - **Selection of pacing mode and system** - mode that maintains AV synchrony and reduces complications of pacing:
      - <u>Physiological ventricular pacing</u> (cardiac resynchronization therapy \[CRT\]):
        - Additional pacing lead in lateral or anterolateral branch of coronary sinus or a single lead in His position (individualsed to the diseased conduction system)
        - Simultaneous RV and lateral LV pacing leading to more physiological LV contraction
